<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis II, Count of Flanders</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1330">1330</a>–<a href="/years/1384">1384</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Louis of Male · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-dampierre">House of Dampierre</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis II, known as Louis of Male, was count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> from 1346 to 1384 and also count of Nevers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rethel">Rethel</a>. He steered between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> and Mechelen from Brabant, and faced a long revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>, which French help defeated at Roosebeke in 1382. The marriage of his heiress Margaret to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a>, duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, brought Flanders into the Burgundian state on his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-ii-count-of-flanders.jpg" alt="Louis II, Count of Flanders" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis de Male (1330-1384; comte de Flandre). Autorité émettrice de monnaie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monnaie_-_Pays-Bas,_Flandre,_Louis_II_de_Male,_royal_-_btv1b11341332k_(1_of_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Louis was born on 25 October 1330, the son of Count Louis I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and Margaret I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, a daughter of King Philip V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and was baptised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vi">Pierre Roger</a>, the future Pope Clement VI. He was wounded at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy">Battle of Crécy</a> on 26 August 1346, where he saw his father killed, and was knighted the same day by Philip VI. The weavers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> pressed him to marry an English bride, but he escaped under cover of a falcon hunt and in 1347 married Margaret of Brabant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> revolted at once, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> of 1348 and the rivalry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypres">Ypres</a> weakened the rising; Louis signed a neutrality treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on 25 August 1348, and on 13 January 1349 the weavers were crushed by the fullers and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>. Thirty years of relative peace followed, kept by balancing between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and England. In 1356 he invaded Brabant over his wife's dowry and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-scheut">Battle of Scheut</a>, and the Peace of Ath of 4 June 1357 gave him Mechelen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>.</p><p>At home he added the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franc-of-bruges">Franc of Bruges</a>, the countryside around the port, to the three traditional members of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, divided his council into specialised bodies and dug a canal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>, his main residence, to the Lys. He lived lavishly, kept a zoo and fathered at least thirteen illegitimate children, and his rising taxes angered the cities. In 1369 he married his heiress Margaret to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a> in return for the restoration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douai">Douai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orchies">Orchies</a> to the county.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Revolt of the White Hoods broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> in 1379. Louis won at Nevele in 1381 but fled when Philip van Artevelde's Ghent forces attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> at Beverhoutsveld on 3 May 1382, and the rebels were crushed only by the royal army of Charles VI at Roosebeke. He died on 30 January 1384 and was buried beside his wife in Saint Peter's Collegiate Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a>. His administrative work laid the ground for the Burgundian rulers who followed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis II of Flanders” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1369 Margaret of Male, heiress of Count <b>Louis of Male</b>, married Philip the Bold, duke of Burgundy and brother of Charles V of France, and on Louis’s death in 1384 Flanders became part of the Burgundian State.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…death of Duke John III in 1355 without a male heir provoked the War of the Brabantine Succession: <b>Louis of Male</b>, count of Flanders, defeated the Brabanters at the Battle of Scheut in August 1356 and took Brussels, until a small band under Everard t'Serclaes recaptured the city…</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Male, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.21,3.28&amp;year=1365" aria-label="Male on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4624 389 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Louis II, Count of Flanders"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5074 659h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5074 659h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.21,3.28&amp;year=1365">What was here in 1365? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
